dents have been  reported to the epa  ranging from skin irritation to seizures  plus  nearly 1700 pet deaths senior scientist  nathan donnelly  the biggest thing that stuck out to me  was just how high these numbers are  elanco disputes that saying the incident  report rate in the u.s has been below  0.3 percent  and the majority relate to non-serious  effects such as skin  problems a company official said that a  report is not an indication of cause  and said there's no established link  between exposure to the active  ingredients in suresto and pet deaths  but congressman raja krishnamoorthy  wants the callers off the market  at least temporarily i think that it's  only  appropriate in this case that the  manufacturer  do a voluntary recall the situation has  to be investigated  and that's why we're taking action as we  are right now  well the company says it's cooperating  with the subcommittee but will not  be uh hosting a voluntary recall that it  says would be up to regulators  the epa says it's in conversation with  elinco about these  incidents um and it should be noted that  many veterinarians continue to recommend  these products now if you have a story  to tell us about this issue or  anything else you can email us at  consumer cbsnews.com  tony you can really feel for those pet  owners we hope we get to the bottom of  this anna thank you very much
 

